
/*

Automatically repairs only your most fragmented subboards, skipping
the others.

RR_Check lists 6 pieces of info about each sub:
 1) Physical sub #.
 2) Number of "holes" that still remain after old msgs have been killed.
 3) Whether area is BASE or UDBASE.
 4) Path to that sub's data-files.
 5) Title of sub.
 6) Overall size of data "_Text" file.

Item #2 will show you which subs need to be RR'ed.
Item #6 will show which subs are consuming the most HD space overall.

>Phys# Holes Type Path               Title                             Size
> 158)    26 BASE M:9.9/             Fido Amiga: Tech. Hardware       1689K
> 160)     2 BASE M:9.11/            Fido Amiga: Arexx                 638K
> 162)     1 BASE M:9.13/            Fido Amiga: SAS/Lattice C         642K
> 172)    12 BASE M:9.1.4/           Fido Amiga BBS: CNet             2698K
> 190)    58 BASE M:15.1/            Non-Comp Fido: Film              2586K
> 193)     1 BASE M:15.4/            Non-Comp Fido: Satellite TV       128K
> 194)     1 BASE M:15.5/            Non-Comp Fido: Broadcast TV        64K
> 195)     5 BASE M:15.6/            Non-Comp Fido: Pro-Video          379K
> Total  110                                                         11092K

You can also specify a different style of output with the "OUTP=2" option:
> Threads Resps Full KBytes Byte/Msg Del-Day High-# Name
>    220    720  99%   866K    942     400      896 Fido Amiga: Worldwide
>    247   1531  55%  1896K   1091      12    16837 Fido Amiga: Tech. (Hardware)
>    156    777  35%   921K   1010     200     1592 Fido Amiga: Arexx
>    134    634  40%   950K   1265     122     2671 Fido Amiga: SAS/Lattice C
>    101    830  22%   953K   1047      35     4488 Fido Amiga: Public Domain
>     79    135  17%   168K    800      20     4249 Fido Amiga: For Sale #1
>    146    957  65%  1019K    945       0     1589 Fido Amiga: Telecomm #1
>     56    291  12%   695K   2045      33     5359 Fido Amiga: TrapDoor FDoor

You can also run RR_Check as an event to automatically repair the subs
that get listed.

Runs online, or from the CLI.

CLI Use:  (Acts only as a subboard-lister, no rebuilding is done.)
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=0 outP=2  ; List all subs
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=1         ; Only list subs with "holes"
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=25        ; List subs with at least 25 "holes"
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=5 maxH=25 ; List subs with 5-25 "holes"

CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minK=100     ; List subs with at least 100K data-files
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minK=100 maxK=250 ; List subs with 100-250K data-files
CLI> rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minK=100 maxB=99  ; List subs with <99 block data-files

CLI> Alias RRH rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=10  ; Make an easy-to-use CLI alias
CLI> Alias RRS rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=0 minK=100 Outp=2

You can also create a DOS-script containing 2 lines like:
CLI> rx CNet:rexx/RR_Check.rexx minH=0 outP=2 >t:1
CLI> c:Sort t:1 * COLSTART 15    ; Sort by "percent full"

Or Online Installation:
   1) Edit CNet:BBSMENU.
      Onto the END of the cmd-list called "1; Maintenance menu" add:
      > RRUdbases | {#1    Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx "minH=1  maxR=999 UDBASE"}
      > RRRebuild | {#1    Pfiles:RR_check.rexx "minH=10 maxR=999"}
                            (Only the last 2 need the double-quotes.)

      If you wish to view the logs, you can optionally also add:
      > GAin | {$0 Grep >t:1 -h " gain" SysData:log/Calls SysData:log/Port*} {*0 t:1}

      Onto the END of the cmd-list called "2; Available everywhere" add:
      > SIZes     | {#5 rx Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=0  Outp=2}
      > RRHoles   | {#5 rx Pfiles:RR_check.rexx minH=25 OutP=2}
      > RRSizes   | {#5 rx Pfiles:RR_check.rexx minH=0  minK=1000}

   2) Edit the variables below marked "SysOp-settable" items.

   3) Copy RR_Check.rexx TO pfiles:RR_Check.rexx

When running from the CLI, you can also re-direct RR_Check's output to
a file, and then sort it based on fragmentation levels.
CLI> rx  >t:RR.tmp Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx minH=1
CLI> Sort t:RR.tmp * COLSTART 6

Or it can be sorted based on data-file (_Text) size:
CLI> Sort t:RR.tmp * COLSTART 72

For automatic event installation:
   1) Set-up a CNet v3.05 CONFIG/EVENT as:
         Ports   : 0
         Weekdays: SMTWRFS
         Time    : 100
         Valid   : 1
         Interval: 0
         Iterate : 1
         Type    : Immediate--force system idle
         Command : RunArexx
         Args    : Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx "minH=50 maxR=9999"
   That will run this pfile at 1am every morning forcing the person on
   port #0 to log-off first.  (Don't forget the quotes.)
   Feel free to change days/times/ports/etc as needed.

   All subs that have more than 50 "holes" in them will be repaired.
   (You can also set the "maxR" variable to limit the max # of subs
   that will be repaired per night.)

   You can also use:
   > Args : Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx "minK=100 maxR=9999"
   which would repair all subs with data-files larger than 100K.
   (Regardless of their fragmented or non-fragmented status.)

If you wish to add it to your pfile-menu...
> Add as AREXX script
> Filename   : Pfiles:RR_Check.rexx
> Description: Just RR fragmented subs
> Args       : "maxR=3 minH=20"     (Don't forget the double-quotes.)
> Acc groups : 23-31                (or whatever)
(That should RR 3 of your subs that have at least 20 holes in them.)

================================================================

Options allowed:
   MINH=#    :  Only subs that have at least n holes.
   MAXH=#    :  Only subs that have at most  n holes.

   MINK=#    :  Only subs that have at least n Kbyte data-files.
   MAXK=#    :  Only subs that have at most  n Kbyte data-files.

   MINB=#    :  Only subs that have at least n block data-files.
   MAXB=#    :  Only subs that have at most  n block data-files.

   MAXR=#    :  Only repair (at most) n subs.
             :  ("MAXR" is what tells RR_check to actually do rebuilding.)

   TEST      :  Just test options, don't do any RR'ing.

   BASE      :  Only effect bases.
   UDBA      :  Only effect UDbases.

   OUTP=#    :  Display output-list in style #1 or #2 format.

================================================================

From the CLI you can use any combination of minH maxH minK maxK MinB MaxB TEST options.

From an online event you can use any comination of minH maxH minK maxK
MinB MaxB maxR TEST options.  ("maxR=#" is what triggers the 'repairing' option.)

The TEST option allows you to test your option-string event set-up
without actually doing any 'repairing'.

If you run RR_Check online, and want it to write to the logs, make
sure you have your Config/Logs/ArexxSays and that user's log-flags
turned on.

You can file-requ "RR_Check" from 1:2410/207 and always be guaranteed
to get the latest release.

